{
	"duration": 52.557,
	"scenes": [
		{
			"id": "s1",
			"start": 0,
			"end": 5.643,
			"text": "On most blockchains, anyone who knows an address can read its balance and every amount it paid."
		},
		{
			"id": "s2",
			"start": 5.643,
			"end": 17.904,
			"text": "On Aztec, each token has two balances. Nulo handles both for you. Your private balance: only you can see it. Your public balance: anyone can see it, like a balance on Ethereum."
		},
		{
			"id": "s3",
			"start": 17.904,
			"end": 27.948,
			"text": "Your private balance is a set of encrypted notes, like cash in sealed envelopes. The network keeps a fingerprint of each note, and a sealed copy only its owner can open."
		},
		{
			"id": "s4",
			"start": 27.948,
			"end": 44.278,
			"text": "Say you pay 3.50 from a note worth 5. Nulo spends that note and makes new ones: 3.50 for the person you pay, and 1.50 change for you. In a private payment, the record shows that something was spent. It does not show which note, or how much."
		},
		{
			"id": "s5",
			"start": 44.278,
			"end": 52.557,
			"text": "Moving money to your public balance is your choice, and the public side records the amount. Nulo labels each balance, so you always know which is which."
		}
	],
